<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="300" d:DesignHeight="700"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.MacroInspectorPanelView"
             x:DataType="vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel">
  <!--
    The key inspector's Macro panel (mockup 2i) — "selecting a key edits its macro right here; the
    Macros tab is a library, not the editor".

    THE RAIL IS 300px WIDE WHILE THIS PANEL IS SHOWING, not 268. The handoff states both widths and
    this is the macro-editing variant; the rail sets it off the panel's own WantsWideRail, so this
    file names no width at all.

    THERE IS NO ASSIGN BUTTON. Editing in place means the first recorded keystroke IS the macro —
    the view model creates it and puts it in the key's first free slot. The old panel's
    draft-then-assign pair was a modal's shape.

    NOTHING HERE IS A GLYPH STANDING IN FOR AN ICON. The record dot is an Ellipse, the drag grip is
    `IconDragHandle` and the insert affordance `IconInsert`: U+25CF, U+283F and U+FF0B are in
    neither embedded IBM Plex family and would tofu. The row's `×` (U+00D7) and the middle dots
    (U+00B7) ARE characters — both families carry them.

    MONO IS THE FILE'S VOICE. The step tokens, the modifier codes, the millisecond counts and the
    meter readings are values that exist verbatim in a config file or are counted against one;
    every sentence the app says in its own voice is sans.
  -->
  <StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== The refusal =====================================================================
         Drawn INSTEAD of the editor, never beside it. A device with no macros, or a position that
         can never carry one (05 §5.3, spec 02's verbatim wording), is the design's sanctioned
         exception to "absent features are not shown": the user pressed this tab, and a tab that
         rendered nothing would look broken. Amber, never red — nothing failed. -->
    <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding !IsAvailable}">
      <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
              BorderThickness="1"
              CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
              Padding="9,7">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding UnavailableReason}"
                   Classes="body statusWarning"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </Border>
    </Border>

    <StackPanel IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable}">

      <!-- ===== The name ======================================================================
           "The macro is picked by name from a dropdown — Sign-off block ▾ — with the reuse note
           'Named, so it can be picked for another key from this same dropdown. Also on [f7] · Fn.'"

           PICKING IS ASSIGNING; RENAMING IS THE MACROS TAB'S. 2i splits the two deliberately, so
           there is no name field here — only the list of what the profile already holds. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.NameLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

          <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding NameOptions}"
                    SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedName, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch">
            <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroNameOptionViewModel">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ComboBox>

          <!-- Said only once the macro really carries a name, because that is exactly what makes
               it pickable for a second key. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.ReuseNote}"
                     Classes="body muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding IsNamed}" />

          <!-- Where else this same macro fires from, built from the library entry's other sites. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding AlsoOnText}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding HasAlsoOnText}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The steps =====================================================================
           Headed `Steps`, with `drag ⠿ · ⌥↑↓` as the reorder affordance and `● Record` beside it. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.SectionTitle}"
                       Classes="cardTitle"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <!-- `drag ⠿ · ⌥↑↓` — the word, the grip drawn as geometry, and the keyboard spelling
                 for this platform.

                 BOTH marks are DRAWN, not typed: U+283F (the grip) and U+2325 (Option) are in
                 neither embedded IBM Plex family, so either character would be tofu. The ⌥ follows
                 the layer chips exactly (issue #109) — `IconOption` beside the text, hidden off
                 macOS where the text itself reads `Alt+`. An Icon paints from Stroke and cannot
                 inherit Foreground, which is what `Icon.kbdMark` is for. -->
            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1"
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        Spacing="3"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ReorderHintPrefix}" Classes="meta muted" />
              <controls:Icon Classes="dragHandle" Data="{DynamicResource IconDragHandle}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <controls:Icon Classes="kbdMark"
                             Data="{DynamicResource IconOption}"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Steps.ShowsOptionMark}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.ReorderShortcut}" Classes="meta muted" />
            </StackPanel>

            <Button Grid.Column="2"
                    Classes="recordAction"
                    Command="{Binding RecordCommand}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Ellipse Classes="recordDot" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <!-- The 1.4s pulse (DurationRecordingPulse), reached by the class the listening cap
                     and the Tap & hold panel already share. Bound, so it runs only while this panel
                     is the one capturing. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordCommandCaption}"
                           Classes.recording="{Binding IsRecording}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Button>
          </Grid>

          <!-- The live banner, naming the step the next keystroke lands in. Amber: it is the app
               telling the user their keyboard has been taken over, not an error. -->
          <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
                  BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="1"
                  CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                  Padding="9,7"
                  IsVisible="{Binding IsRecording}">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordingBanner}" Classes="body statusWarning" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
          </Border>

          <!-- What the capture does with what it hears, verbatim from 2i. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.CaptureRule}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />

          <!-- The rows. An ItemsControl and NOT a ListBox, deliberately: the editor's grammar
               leaves the arrows to any SelectingItemsControl in the focused ancestry, so a
               selecting list here would swallow the very ⌥↑↓ the header advertises. Selection is
               the view model's own. -->
          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Steps.Items}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroInspectorStepViewModel">
                <!-- The row is a Grid rather than one big Button, and the delete and delay actions
                     sit BESIDE the selecting button rather than inside it. Two reasons, and both
                     have bitten this codebase: a button nested in a button fires both commands on
                     one click, and a `$parent[...]` binding written inside a themed button's
                     content no longer reaches past the presenter (docs/app/design-system.md,
                     "The control-theme layer"). Every ancestor binding here is therefore anchored
                     on this view, whose DataContext is the panel. -->
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto"
                      ColumnSpacing="4"
                      Background="Transparent">
                  <controls:Icon Grid.Column="0"
                                 Classes="dragHandle"
                                 Data="{DynamicResource IconDragHandle}"
                                 ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ReorderHandleHint}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                  <Button Grid.Column="1"
                          Classes="macroStepRow"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.SelectStepCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <!-- Mono end to end: the number counts the file's own steps and the token is
                           literally what layoutN.txt carries. -->
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding NumberText}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                      <!-- THE KEY THAT WAS STRUCK IS TINTED. `MacroStepKeyBrush` is the macro
                           badge's own orchid, darkened on light for type — purple already means
                           "macro" in this app, so the cap's dot and this token say one thing in one
                           hue, and the row invents no fifth meaning. Deliberately not the accent:
                           that has exactly two, and a key token is neither. -->
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding TokenText}"
                                 Classes="monoValue"
                                 Foreground="{DynamicResource MacroStepKeyBrush}"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasToken}"
                                 TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                      <!-- The held modifiers as 2i's marks — `⇧`, `R⌃` — and NOT the file's `LS`,
                           `LC`, `S ` codes, which are what goes on the drive rather than what a
                           person reads (`Keystroke.FormatModifiers`, untouched).

                           LEFT IS UNMARKED; ONLY THE RIGHT SIDE IS SPELLED. `LS` draws a bare `⇧`
                           and `RS` draws `R⇧`, so the side run below is hidden for a left modifier
                           — and for a generic one, which is why the two now draw alike. See
                           `MacroModifierMarks.LeftSide` for the reasoning and the cost.

                           UP TO TWO RUNS PER MODIFIER, IN TWO FACES. The key-symbol family is a
                           19-codepoint subset with NO Latin letters, so the `R` side letter stays
                           in the ordinary mono face and only the mark goes in `.keySymbol`; a whole
                           `R⇧` set in that class would fall back to a system font for its `R`.
                           `MacroModifierMarks.Split` hands the two out separately for exactly this,
                           and the row binds to the parts rather than to the joined string. -->
                      <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Modifiers}"
                                    IsVisible="{Binding HasModifiers}"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                          <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3" />
                          </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                          <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroModifierMarks+Mark">
                            <!-- The tooltip is not decoration: only a RIGHT modifier spells its
                                 side, so a bare mark is worn by the left one and the generic one
                                 alike and the row cannot tell them apart. `Description` is where
                                 the distinction the file really carries stays reachable. -->
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                        ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}">
                              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                                         Classes="monoValueSmall muted"
                                         IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                                         Classes="keySymbol muted"
                                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>
                          </DataTemplate>
                        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      </ItemsControl>

                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding ActionText}" Classes="meta muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>

                  <!-- The per-step delay, edited in place — this is where §11.3's dialog went. A
                       row with none offers `delay…`; a row with one shows it in mono, because
                       `80 ms` is a `{d080}` token in the macro.

                       GHOST, NOT LINK. It was a `link` until a captured frame showed what that
                       costs: one accent-coloured affordance per row, three of them stacked beside a
                       red Record, against a design law that gives the accent exactly two meanings
                       (selection/focus, and "you changed this"). A per-row affordance is neither.
                       Chromeless and muted, like the `×` beside it. -->
                  <Button Grid.Column="2"
                          Classes="ghost"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.EditDelayCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3">
                      <TextBlock Text="·" Classes="meta muted" IsVisible="{Binding HasDelay}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding DelayText}" Classes="monoValueSmall" IsVisible="{Binding HasDelay}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.AddDelayCaption}"
                                 Classes="meta muted"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding !HasDelay}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>

                  <Button Grid.Column="3"
                          Classes="ghost"
                          Content="×"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.RemoveStepHint}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.RemoveStepCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}" />

                  <!-- WHERE THE DRAG WOULD LAND. A reorder with no feedback reads as broken even
                       once it works, so the row under a carried step wears a ring while the
                       pointer is over it.

                       A RING, NOT A FILL: the selected row already takes `AccentSelectionFill`, and
                       a drop target painted in it would be indistinguishable from the selection it
                       is about to displace. Last child and `IsHitTestVisible=False`, so it draws
                       over the row button's own face without ever becoming the thing the release
                       hit-tests to. -->
                  <Border Grid.Column="0"
                          Grid.ColumnSpan="4"
                          BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
                          BorderThickness="1"
                          CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                          IsHitTestVisible="False"
                          IsVisible="{Binding IsDropTarget}" />
                </Grid>
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>

          <!-- The trailing row of 2i's list. It runs the same action as `● Record` — a step's
               content is a keystroke, and recording one is how it is made — and the number is the
               step the banner will name. -->
          <Button Classes="macroStepRow" Command="{Binding InsertStepCommand}">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.NextStepNumberText}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <controls:Icon Classes="insertMark" Data="{DynamicResource IconInsert}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.InsertStepCaption}"
                         Classes="meta"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Button>

          <!-- ===== §11.3, with its window taken off ==========================================
               The random radio, the custom 1-999 field with its clamped steppers, and §11.3's own
               validation sentence — over the row the delay belongs to, rather than in a modal over
               a board the user cannot see any more. -->
          <Border Classes="macroDelayEditor" IsVisible="{Binding Steps.IsDelayEditorOpen}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.DelaySectionTitle}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

              <!-- The firmware gate answered IN PLACE, exactly as the Tap & hold panel answers
                   its own: a refusal for a surface already on screen is an interruption with
                   nothing to interrupt. -->
              <StackPanel Spacing="8" IsVisible="{Binding !Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.DelayUnavailableReason}"
                           Classes="body statusWarning"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                <Button Classes="secondary"
                        Content="{Binding Steps.UpdateFirmwareCaption}"
                        Command="{Binding Steps.UpdateFirmwareCommand}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Steps.CanUpdateFirmware}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
              </StackPanel>

              <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsVisible="{Binding Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}">
                <!-- The caption is a TextBlock on the type scale rather than a bare string: a
                     RadioButton's own content takes Fluent's default step, which a frame showed
                     reading a size larger than everything around it in the rail. -->
                <RadioButton IsChecked="{Binding Steps.IsRandomDelay, Mode=TwoWay}">
                  <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.RandomDelayCaption}" Classes="body" />
                </RadioButton>

                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.CustomDelayCaption}" Classes="meta muted" />

                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
                  <!-- A real TextBox, and that is correct here: focus landing in one ENDS the
                       recording — the editor's `GotFocus` leg of `StopRecordingOnInteraction`
                       stands the panel down and the banner goes with it — so the digits reach the
                       field and the panel is never left claiming to record while the keyboard
                       belongs to the box (docs/app/keystroke-capture.md, invariant 7: a surface
                       that says it is capturing must never be wrong about it). The service's own
                       auto-suspend on text focus still applies underneath, but it is silent, and a
                       silent suspension is exactly what this panel must not rely on.

                       It is the opposite of an action field, which records a KEYPRESS and must
                       never be focusable. Mono — the count is written into the macro verbatim.

                       The steppers are captioned `+`/`-` rather than `▲`/`▼`: the triangles are
                       not in either embedded family, and an arithmetic sign is not an icon
                       standing in for a mark. -->
                  <TextBox Grid.Column="0"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           Text="{Binding Steps.CustomDelayMilliseconds, Mode=TwoWay}" />
                  <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                    <Button Content="+" MinWidth="32" Command="{Binding Steps.IncreaseDelayCommand}" />
                    <Button Content="-" MinWidth="32" Command="{Binding Steps.DecreaseDelayCommand}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Grid>

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.DelayError}"
                           Classes="body statusError"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           IsVisible="{Binding Steps.HasDelayError}" />

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                  <Button Classes="primaryAction"
                          Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ApplyDelayCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.ApplyDelayCommand}" />
                  <Button Classes="secondary"
                          Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ClearDelayCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.ClearDelayCommand}" />
                  <Button Classes="secondary"
                          Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.CloseDelayCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.CloseDelayCommand}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- What the panel refused, and why. Amber: a budget or a full slot strip is a fact about
               the profile, not a failure. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding Message}"
                     Classes="body statusWarning"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The footer meters =============================================================
           2i's three readings, in its order: `Playback speed 3 / 5`, `this macro 128 / 500`,
           `layout keystrokes 5 140 / 7 200`. Over budget goes AMBER and still saves — the layout
           is written as it stands and the board truncates. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <StackPanel Spacing="4">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeed}">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding SpeedMeter.Label}" Classes="body" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding SpeedMeter.Caption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Classes.statusWarning="{Binding SpeedMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding MacroLengthMeter.Label}" Classes="body" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding MacroLengthMeter.Caption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Classes.statusWarning="{Binding MacroLengthMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.Label}" Classes="body" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.Caption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Classes.statusWarning="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
          </StackPanel>

          <!-- Speed itself, beside the reading that counts it. -->
          <Slider Minimum="{Binding SpeedMinimum}"
                  Maximum="{Binding SpeedMaximum}"
                  Value="{Binding Speed, Mode=TwoWay}"
                  TickFrequency="1"
                  IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
                  IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeed}" />

          <!-- REPEAT AND THE CO-TRIGGERS STAY. Mockup 2i draws only speed; both of these are
               supported by the devices this app edits today and there is nowhere else left to set
               them once the Macros tab becomes a library, so dropping them would be dropping a
               feature silently. Recorded as a deviation in docs/app/design-system.md.

               Absent, never disabled, where the file does not keep them: the Advantage2 has a
               repeat range in the model but its serializer writes no {xN} token at all. -->
          <StackPanel Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding HasRepeat}">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                         Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.RepeatLabel}"
                         Classes="body"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Repeat}" Classes="monoValue" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>
            <Slider Minimum="{Binding RepeatMinimum}"
                    Maximum="{Binding RepeatMaximum}"
                    Value="{Binding Repeat, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    TickFrequency="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" />
          </StackPanel>

          <StackPanel Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding HasCoTriggers}">
            <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.CoTriggersLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding CoTriggers}">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <WrapPanel />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <!-- THE SIX TOGGLES ARE MARKS, NOT CAPTIONS — `⇧ R⇧ ⌃ R⌃ ⌥ R⌥`. Six two-line
                   captions (`Left⏎Shift` … `Right⏎Opt`) wrapped in a rail this narrow was the
                   panel's tallest block for the least information in it; the marks are the step
                   list's own spelling, one line each, and they cost nothing to read because the
                   modifier is the whole of what the toggle says. With left unmarked they need
                   260 px of the strip's 276 and all six sit on ONE row.

                   UP TO TWO RUNS, IN TWO FACES, exactly as the step row above draws them: the
                   key-symbol family is a 19-codepoint subset with NO Latin letters, so the `R`
                   stays in the ordinary mono face and only the mark goes in `.keySymbol`. The left
                   three spell no side and are a single run. `FontSizeKeySymbol` is derived to sit
                   at `monoValueSmall`'s cap height, which is why that is the pairing here too.

                   NEITHER RUN CARRIES A COLOUR, and that is the reason this site can be type at all
                   while the layer chips' `⌥` stays a geometry: `ToggleSegment`'s `.selected` face
                   sets `Foreground`, a TextBlock INHERITS it and an `Icon` (which paints from
                   `Stroke`) cannot. A `muted` on either run would break the on-accent label.

                   THE CAPTION IS THE TOOLTIP. A mark alone is not accessible text; with left
                   unmarked it is also the only thing on a left toggle that says which side it is;
                   and it is the fallback for any co-trigger key that names no modifier at all. -->
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroCoTriggerViewModel">
                  <Button Classes="toggleSegment"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                          Margin="0,0,4,4"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).ToggleCoTriggerCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                                 Classes="monoValueSmall"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                                 Classes="keySymbol"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasMark}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding !HasMark}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
          </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
